      THE OPENBOOKS PLUGIN-API LINKING EXCEPTION, VERSION 1.0
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Copyright (C) 2026 Ian Berryman and the OpenBooks contributors

The text above is the Apache License, Version 2.0, unmodified. The text below
is an additional permission that accompanies it. It grants rights; it takes
none away. If you rely only on the Apache License above, you do not need to
read further.

WHY THIS EXCEPTION EXISTS

  The OpenBooks server is licensed under the GNU Affero General Public
  License, version 3 (AGPL-3.0). This package -- @openbooks/plugin-api -- and
  the published OpenBooks OpenAPI description are licensed under the Apache
  License 2.0 instead, because they are the contract other software is meant
  to be written against.

  Absent an explicit statement, it is not obvious whether a module written
  against the interfaces declared here becomes a work based on the
  AGPL-licensed server merely by declaring that dependence. This exception
  answers that question. It does not answer the separate question of what
  happens when such a module is combined with the server and that combination
  is conveyed or operated; section 3(b) is explicit that no permission is
  granted there.

1. DEFINITIONS

  1.1 "The Contract" means this package (@openbooks/plugin-api) and the
      OpenBooks OpenAPI description published by the OpenBooks project, in
      each case as distributed by the OpenBooks project under the Apache
      License 2.0 -- that is, the declared interfaces, types, schemas,
      constants, and documentation that describe how software interacts with
      OpenBooks.

  1.2 "The Server" means those parts of OpenBooks that the OpenBooks project
      distributes under the AGPL-3.0, and every modified version of them.

  1.3 "Your Module" means a work of authorship that:

        (a) is separate from The Server and contains no source code or object
            code of The Server, and no work derived from The Server, other
            than material from The Contract; and

        (b) interacts with OpenBooks only by implementing, consuming,
            importing, or otherwise conforming to declarations in The
            Contract, and by data passed across those declarations.

2. ADDITIONAL PERMISSION GRANTED

  As an additional permission under section 7 of the AGPL-3.0, the copyright
  holders of The Server grant you the following permission with respect to
  The Server:

  2.1 Writing, compiling, testing, packaging, distributing, and offering for
      sale Your Module does not, by itself, make Your Module a modified
      version of The Server or a work based on The Server, and does not
      subject Your Module to the AGPL-3.0. This holds whether the dependence
      on The Contract is expressed as a package dependency, as compiled-in
      type or interface declarations, as generated client code, or as
      conformance to a schema.

  2.2 You may therefore license and convey Your Module on terms of your own
      choosing, including terms that are proprietary and terms under which
      you do not make source code available.

  2.3 For the avoidance of doubt, the permission in 2.1 covers Your Module
      standing alone. Nothing in it is conditioned on your also holding a
      license to The Server.

3. WHAT THIS EXCEPTION DOES NOT GRANT

  This exception is narrow by design. In particular:

  3.1 (a) No exception to The Server. The Server remains subject to the
      AGPL-3.0 in full, including section 5 (conveying modified source
      versions) and section 13 (remote network interaction -- the obligation
      to offer Corresponding Source to users who interact with a modified
      Server over a network). Nothing here waives, narrows, defers, or
      creates an alternative to section 13 for The Server.

  3.2 (b) This is NOT a general linking exception. It grants no permission to
      convey -- or to make available to users over a network -- a combined
      work consisting of The Server together with Your Module loaded into it,
      on any terms other than the AGPL-3.0. If you build such a combination
      and convey or operate it, the AGPL-3.0 governs the combination,
      including its source-offer obligations.

      This limit is deliberate. OpenBooks intends third-party products to
      integrate as external clients of its public HTTP API and OAuth
      authorization server, where no combination with AGPL-licensed code
      occurs (see section 4), rather than as modules bundled into the server
      process. In-process modules and API clients are not equivalent
      positions under this license, and this exception does not make them
      equivalent.

  3.3 (c) No coverage for a work that reaches past The Contract. If a work
      copies, adapts, translates, or incorporates any part of The Server
      beyond The Contract, imports server-internal modules, reads or writes
      the OpenBooks database schema directly, or otherwise depends on
      internals that The Contract does not declare, it is not Your Module
      within the meaning of section 1.3, this exception does not apply to it,
      and the AGPL-3.0 applies in the ordinary way.

  3.4 (d) No change to The Server's license. Combining Your Module with The
      Server does not relicense The Server, and creates no obligation on the
      OpenBooks project to accept, support, document, or preserve
      compatibility with Your Module.

  3.5 (e) No trademark license. Neither the Apache License above (see its
      section 6) nor this exception grants any right to use the OpenBooks
      name, logo, or other marks, including in a way that suggests
      endorsement of Your Module.

4. INTERACTION OVER THE PUBLIC API

  For clarity, and consistent with the design of OpenBooks: a program that
  interacts with a running OpenBooks instance only over its public HTTP API
  or its MCP surface -- as a separate program communicating across a process
  or network boundary, holding credentials issued to it as a client -- is not
  a combined work with The Server. It requires no permission from the
  OpenBooks copyright holders, and neither the AGPL-3.0 nor this exception
  imposes any obligation on it.

5. RELATIONSHIP TO THE APACHE LICENSE, AND SEVERABILITY

  Your rights in The Contract itself arise from the Apache License 2.0 above
  and are not conditioned on this exception. If any provision of this
  exception is held unenforceable, that holding does not affect the Apache
  License 2.0 grant covering The Contract, nor the AGPL-3.0 covering The
  Server; the remaining provisions of this exception continue in effect.

6. VERSIONING

  This is version 1.0 of the exception. The OpenBooks copyright holders may
  publish revised versions. A revised version applies only to distributions
  that carry it; the version accompanying a given distribution of The
  Contract governs that distribution, and permission already granted under
  this version for Your Module is not withdrawn by a later version.

